% A document with NO CJK in it, which is what makes it worth having: every
% other fixture and every corpus document is Japanese, and the `--format
% latex` preamble takes a different branch when it sees no CJK — no
% `luatexja`, an `iftex`-guarded `fontenc` instead, and a claim in a comment
% that the file compiles under pdflatex, xelatex AND lualatex alike.
% `latex_output.rs` compiles it under all three, so the claim is checked
% rather than asserted.
%
% The prose is chosen for what it forces through the escaper. Every character
% LaTeX reserves is in it, written with SATySFi's own `\`-escape, and the
% failure modes are not cosmetic: an unescaped `%` comments out the rest of
% the line and the file STILL COMPILES, so a document that says "100% of
% them" would silently lose the other half of its sentence and nothing would
% report it.

@require: stdja-mini
@require: math
@require: annot
@require: itemize

document (|
  title = {Escaping, math and structure with no CJK in sight};
  author = {rustyfi};
|) '<
  +p {
    Reserved characters, all of them: 100\% of the budget, \#3 in the series,
    a \& b, x_1, \{braces\}, ~tilde, ^caret, and a back\\slash. Also
    \<angles\> and a \|pipe\|, which are only correct under a stated font
    encoding.
  }
  +p {
    Mathematics: ${\frac{a + b}{c}}, then ${\sum_{k=1}^{n} k^2}, then
    ${\alpha + \beta \leq \gamma}.
  }
  +p {
    A link: \href?*(`https://example.invalid/a%20b#frag`){percent-encoded},
    whose URL must keep its escaping or take the rest of the line with it.
  }
  +listing{
    * first item
    * second item
  }
  +p { After the list. }
>
